+The compatibility view itself is sound, provided it exposes every column as a plain reference, including `attempt_id` and `finished_at`. PostgreSQL supports `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `RETURNING`, and `INSERT … ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE` through such a view. The old store does not catch production constraints by name, and old/new writes reach the same physical row. [PostgreSQL’s updatable-view documentation](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-createview.html) confirms this.
+
+1. **P1 — Keep the old API route for the deploy window.**
+
+   **Evidence:** The plan deliberately accepts a 404 for stale tabs ([plan:109](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/docs/plans/261011g-referee-claims-become-promises-and-starter-criteria-wait-for-the-referee-own.md:109>)). The old hook turns a GET 404 into `loadFailed` (`src/web/useClaims.ts:118-153` at HEAD), while a POST 404 first replaces the visible run with an empty pending run and then an error (`src/web/useClaims.ts:179-247`). It does not safely interpret the response as “not made yet.” The direct precedent identified this exact failure as P1 and retained thin aliases ([precedent:390](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/docs/plans/261009w-peer-review-becomes-sources-all-the-way-down.md:390>)).
+
+   **Fix:** Retain old GET and POST routes for one deploy. GET must translate the new outer run fields to `claims`/`claimsOmitted`; POST must translate every SSE frame the old hook reads—`begin`, item event and payload, and `done`—not merely call the new handler unchanged. Add stale-tab tests for both an existing run and a pressed rerun. Name removal in the contract item.
+
+2. **P2 — The old URL needs lifting through navigation and remembered-view restoration, not only initial page load.**
+
+   **Evidence:** `referee` is stored verbatim in localStorage ([last-view.ts:98](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/src/web/last-view.ts:98>)). Restoration happens after boot and explicitly passes through `liftedLegacySearch` ([last-view.ts:469](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/src/web/last-view.ts:469>)); navigation and Back/Forward use `liftedLegacyHref` ([router.ts:1511](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/src/web/router.ts:1511>)). An unlifted `claims` value silently parses to the default Criteria view ([referee-views.ts:81](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/src/web/referee-views.ts:81>)). The plan promises an old-link test only ([plan:186](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/docs/plans/261011g-referee-claims-become-promises-and-starter-criteria-wait-for-the-referee-own.md:186>)).
+
+   **Fix:** Add one pure `claims → promises` query transform and compose it into `settleAddress`, `liftedLegacyHref`, and `liftedLegacySearch`. Test fresh boot, in-app navigation/Back, and restoration of a stored `?referee=claims`.
+
+3. **P2 — “At least one saved row” is the wrong state for the anti-anchoring gate.**
+
+   **Evidence:** The research says “after the reviewer has written” a criterion ([research:153](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/docs/research/261009b-what-a-peer-reviewer-needs-and-where-sources-and-referee-divide.md:153>)), not “while a successful row currently exists.” `useCriteria` inserts an optimistic pending row before acceptance ([useCriteria.ts:251](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/src/web/useCriteria.ts:251>)) and retains failed/refused drafts ([useCriteria.ts:347](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/src/web/useCriteria.ts:347>)). Deletion immediately removes the row ([useCriteria.ts:439](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/src/web/useCriteria.ts:439>)). Re-hiding starters after deletion cannot undo their earlier anchoring and makes the interface flicker between locked and unlocked.
+
+   **Fix:** Use a per-article/session `hasAuthoredCriterion` latch: initialise it from a successfully loaded row or local refusal draft, set it synchronously when the referee submits their own words, and do not clear it when rows are deleted. Before the opening read establishes anything, show neither starters nor a misleading “write one first” message. No chat or MCP path currently creates referee criteria, so provenance machinery is unnecessary now; document that future generated criteria must not unlock the gate automatically.
+
+4. **P2 — “What reviewers often miss” overclaims the evidence, and Allocation contradicts the evidence already in the repo.**
+
+   **Evidence:** The plan admits the study’s error table was not read ([plan:163](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/docs/plans/261011g-referee-claims-become-promises-and-starter-criteria-wait-for-the-referee-own.md:163>)). The research supports the aggregate result and four broad examples, not this exact six-item list ([research:153](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/docs/research/261009b-what-a-peer-reviewer-needs-and-where-sources-and-referee-divide.md:153>)). Its deeper pass specifically says biased randomisation was among the errors reviewers caught best ([web pass:60](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/docs/research/261009b-peer-reviewer-web-pass-sonnet.md:60>)), yet “Allocation” is placed under “What reviewers often miss.”
+
+   **Fix:** Simplest: call the group “Second-pass checks” or “Checks worth making,” and cite the study only for the aggregate observation that reviewers miss many planted errors—not as provenance for these six. Alternatively, read the table and rebuild the group from genuinely low-detection, broadly applicable errors. Remove Allocation from an “often missed” group unless new evidence supports it.
+
+5. **P2 — The ledger alias is assigned to a table that no longer exists.**
+
+   **Evidence:** The plan says to add the mapping to `RENAMED` in `cost-categories.ts` ([plan:38](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/docs/plans/261011g-referee-claims-become-promises-and-starter-criteria-wait-for-the-referee-own.md:38>)). That table was removed; `cost-categories.ts` explicitly points to the single canonicaliser ([cost-categories.ts:329](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/src/cost-categories.ts:329>)). Append-only job aliases now belong in `RETIRED_JOBS` ([step-order.ts:250](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/src/step-order.ts:250>)).
+
+   **Fix:** Add `"referee-claims": "referee-promises"` to `RETIRED_JOBS`. Test the cost category, cost cube, and article-cost readers so old `ai_calls.job` rows retain their classification and display name.
+
+6. **P3 — The final old-name sweep misses slash-form URLs and the `clm-` CSS namespace.**
+
+   **Evidence:** The completion regex ([plan:188](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/docs/plans/261011g-referee-claims-become-promises-and-starter-criteria-wait-for-the-referee-own.md:188>)) does not match `/api/referee/claims`, so active route tests and the deliberate offline-cache exclusion can retain the old spelling ([api.ts:1051](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/src/web/lib/api.ts:1051>), [cache test:320](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/tests/cacheable-covers-artefact-routes.test.ts:320>)). It also misses the extensive `.clm-*` classes ([referee.css:651](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/src/web/styles/referee.css:651>)), although the rename policy explicitly includes CSS classes ([rename-or-move.md:51](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbh5aypq-referee-promises-starters/docs/reusable/rename-or-move.md:51>)). Referee reads are deliberately not cached, so there is no IndexedDB key migration—but the comments and exclusion test still need the new route.
+
+   **Fix:** Add `/api/referee/claims` and `clm-` to the inventory and terminal grep; rename the CSS namespace and its tests. Keep only the intentional compatibility-route occurrences, each tied to the contract removal.
+
+Keeping the inner JSON key `claim` is correct. It names the paper’s assertion, not the feature, and is already the semantic field described by the type (`src/referee-claims.ts:136-158` at HEAD). Renaming it would add a stored-data rewrite and dual reader without making the model wire or domain language clearer.
+
+BUILD WITH CHANGES
